ked the place that you're in  right now  i did and i i you know what i'm looking  for is i don't really want to feel like  i'm traveling  i want to feel like i actually live  somewhere even if i'm there just for a  couple weeks and so i'm looking for a  place in a local community i'm here in  reynoldstown in atlanta this is  incredibly nice home by his really  wonderful host named kimberly and i  really love great design good wi-fi so i  can do interviews things like that  wi-fi is key that's for sure right i  want to talk about a new policy that you  guys are rolling out in oregon um to  fight racial bias i know that there was  a lawsuit settled um tell us about that  policy  well i think just a step back you know  airbnb is all about connecting people  all over the world on new year's eve  four and a half million people were  staying in airbnb so if we're going to  bring people together the opposite of  bringing people together is  discrimination we don't stand for it we  make every member on airbnb sign a  community commitment now one of the  things we did is we worked with the  state of oregon to limit how much  information a guest shows about them so  we don't show their full name or their  photo just their initials we think this  could reduce discrimination of hosts  deciding they don't want to host  somebody because of the color of their  skin or or their name now the key thing  is we're constantly innovating and  testing different ways to reduce  discrimination we have an entire team  dedicated to this and so i think that  for airbnb to work we cannot have  discrimination because that's an  existential threat to the company  how is omicron change the game for you  brian because i got my favorite daughter  kirby just had a little baby and she's  looking for child care and one of the  things that she told me last night is  they're going to look for airbnb to  quarantine someone before they bring  them into the house because omicron is  so off the charts i actually thought  that's a really great idea how has that  changed the game or has omicron changed  the game for you  yeah i mean basically the longer the  pandemic goes on and every new strain  keeps the pandemic going on longer the  more people don't travel the way they  used to they used to go to a city for a  couple days typically for business  travel often staying in a hotel district  and the longer it goes on gail you're  totally right people are now looking to  stay longer almost half our business is  longer than a week a fifth of our  business by nights are people staying  longer than a month we have people  staying for entire seasons they're  obviously quarantining they're getting  away so it's really changing the game  the longer this pandemic goes on the  more the world never goes back to the  way it was and that means travel isn't  going back to the way it was it means  that traveling and living are starting  to blur and people are truly living in  homes all over the world  and what happens if you find something  brian that's not cool in an airbnb  property what you gonna do  what i'm gonna do is get right on the  phone of my team and say we need to  figure out the root causes so no one  else experiences this and i'm already uh  creating a list of ideas based on this  experience and i think that like one of  the big risks of a ceo is you're stuck  in your ivory tower and i feel like you  got to get on the field and be  constantly using your product  well brian that's great brian don't  forget to leave a review it's part of  being part of a community uh we  appreciate you being great to see you  brian being part of our thank you good  to see you very much
 

